# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: lone-cr-in-string-literal -- a lone CR inside a string literal becomes LF in clautolisp
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-27

* Symptom

A line break written literally inside a string literal is accepted by
every engine, and CR LF / LF both become a single LF (code 10)
everywhere. A LONE CR (code 13) inside the string is where they differ:

| reader | clautolisp 2.3.24 | AutoCAD 2022 Windows | BricsCAD V25 Windows | BricsCAD V26 macOS |
|--------+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+--------------------|
| =read= | 10                | 13                   | 13                   | 13                 |
| =load= | 10                | 13                   | 10                   | 10                 |

=load= of ="ab<CR><CR>cd"= in a CR LF file: AutoCAD (13 13), BricsCAD
and clautolisp (10 10).

* Evidence

Probe =probes/sources/probe-multiline-string.lsp= (fixtures in
=probes/sources/fixtures/mls-*.lsp=, byte-exact), run 2026-10-27:

- =probe-results/autocad/ms-windows/20261027T092452Z/=
- =probe-results/bricscad/ms-windows/20261027T092505Z/=
- =probe-results/bricscad/macos/20261027T092421Z/=
- =probe-results/clautolisp/macos/20261027T092420Z/=

* Classification (AGENTS.md, Dialect Divergence)

- =read=: case 0 (all vendors agree: lone CR kept). clautolisp is WRONG
  in every dialect -- =read= on a string is not a file boundary and must
  not normalise line endings.
- =load=: vendors disagree (AutoCAD keeps CR, BricsCAD turns it into LF),
  no vendor document covers it: case 3 (unresolved) until the spec adopts
  one. =--dialect autocad= should keep the CR.

* To do

1. Regression tests (reader) for both readers, then fix =read=.
2. Make the =load= behaviour follow the product under =--dialect=.
3. Add the results to the autolisp-spec "String Literal" reader entry
   (version bump), with the case-3 note.
